# Report: teen-side evidence on parental control apps; LGBTQ+ youth and monitoring; family location sharing as coercive control

Every claim below is tagged **MEASURED** (I read the number in the primary source), **CLAIMED** (secondary source reports it), or **NOT REACHED**.

---

## PART 1 — TEEN-SIDE RESEARCH ON PARENTAL CONTROL APPS

### 1.1 The real "1.7 stars from teens" paper — FOUND, FULL TEXT READ

**Ghosh, A.K., Badillo-Urquiola, K., Guha, S., LaViola, J.J. Jr., Wisniewski, P.J. (2018). "Safety vs. Surveillance: What Children Have to Say about Mobile Apps for Parental Control." CHI 2018, Montreal, April 21–26, 2018.**
- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1145/3173574.3173698 (Crossref: published-online **2018-04-19**)
- Free full-text PDF (the only reachable copy; ACM, UCF STARS and Marquette all returned 403 or metadata-only): **https://www.cs.ucf.edu/icerc/isuelab/publications/pubs/pn1838-ghoshA.pdf** (14pp; also served from `www.eecs.ucf.edu/icerc/...` — note the `/icerc/` path segment; the `/isuelab/` path without it 404s)
- Citations: 181 (Semantic Scholar), 162 (OpenAlex), as of 2026-09-04

**Method — MEASURED:**
- Scraped Google Play reviews in **August 2016** using "Heedzy," seeded from Wisniewski et al.'s (CSCW 2017) list of **75** adolescent online-safety apps. **9 apps had been delisted → 66 apps remained.**
- **29,272 total user reviews** scraped across those 66 apps.
- First author hand-read all ~29K reviews to identify child-authored ones; an undergrad RA re-read the entire set for misses. Keyword seeds: "my parents," "my mom," "my dad," "my father," "I am * old," "years old," "my step" (targeted keyword pass alone surfaced "over 450").
- **Final child dataset: 736 reviews across 37 apps** (29 of the 66 apps had no child reviews at all). Reviewer ages **8–19**. At least **~700 distinct child users** (10 users reviewed multiple apps; 6 reviews anonymous "A Google User").
- No IRB protocol — all data public, no human-subject interaction.
- Review dates: 2012 (<1%), 2013 (3%), 2014 (22%), 2015 (39%), 2016 (36%).
- Concentration: **Screen Time Companion App = 216 reviews (29% of the dataset)**; Qustodio Parental Control and Mobile Fence Parental Control = **80 each (22% combined)**.

**Ratings — MEASURED (this is the origin of the "~1.7 stars" claim):**
- Child star distribution: **1★ = 76%; 2★ = 3%; 3★ = 4%; 4★ = 3%; 5★ = 14%.**
- **Child mean rating M = 1.79, SD = 1.48** (n=736).
- **Remaining (presumed-parent) reviews: N = 28,536, M = 3.66, SD = 1.79.**
- Difference: **parents 1.866 points higher, 95% CI [1.76, 1.98], t(793.34) = 33.77, p < 0.05.**
- Split at ≤2★ = "disliked": **581 low-rated (79%)** vs **155 high-rated (21%)**.

**Coded complaints — MEASURED (percentages are theme frequency; multiple codes per review allowed and double-counted):**

Disliked (79% of reviews):
| Theme | % | Codes |
|---|---|---|
| Overly Restrictive | **35%** | restriction, rebellion, blocking, lack of freedom, oppressive, anger |
| **Privacy Invasive** | **23%** | monitoring, privacy, **stalking**, lack of respect |
| Bad Parenting / Lack of Communication | **14%** | active mediation, upfront, communication, **lazy** |
| Faulty Design & Usability | **14%** | usability, design flaws, performance, bugs |

Liked (21% of reviews):
| Theme | % |
|---|---|
| Control Unhealthy Behaviors | **23%** |
| Kept them Safe | **17%** |
| Ability to Negotiate / More Freedom | **12%** |

Sub-detail: within the "overly restrictive" set, **28%** objected to time restrictions specifically; **21%** of reviews mentioned screen time. Total emergent codes: **24 additional codes** beyond the TOSS template codes, grouped into **7 main themes**.

**On the "abuse" / "emotional abuse" question — MEASURED NEGATIVE RESULT:**
I grepped the full extracted text of the paper. **The string "abuse" appears ZERO times in Ghosh et al. CHI 2018.** There is no count of teens describing monitoring as abusive in this paper. The strongest published language is:
- children "equated the apps to a form of **parental stalking**";
- verbatim teen quotes the authors reproduce: *"I can't even talk to my biological dad, or my boyfriend, or best friend without being stalked by my mom"* (1★, SecureTeen, 2015); *"Awful invasion of privacy! ... Parents should be ashamed of themselves ... This is **evil**!"* (1★, SecureTeen, 2014); *"if you love your kids at all, why don't you try communicating with them instead of buying **spyware**"* (1★, SecureTeen, 2016); *"Now my mom is **stalking** me... Too invasive and down right disrespectful. Thanks for the trust, mom"* (1★, MamaBear, 2014);
- the authors' own strong-language finding: children using "restrict/block/limit" also used **"hate," "horrible," "dumb," "bad," "sucks"** — "they did not simply dislike the apps... **they despised them**."
- Authors' framing: parents "create **asymmetric power differentials**"; apps violate **contextual integrity** (Nissenbaum); apps "may undermine trust and harm children's relationship with their parents, and thus, are **not the ideal solution** for protecting them, at least in their present form."

**⚠️ Correction to a common citation error:** the widely-repeated "1.7 stars" figure is a *mean of teen reviews only* (M=1.79), not an app-store rating. And the paper's own preferred headline number is **76% one-star**, not the mean.

---

### 1.2 "A Matter of Control or Safety?" — the restriction→MORE risk finding

**Ghosh, Badillo-Urquiola, Rosson, Xu, Carroll, Wisniewski (2018). CHI 2018.** DOI https://doi.org/10.1145/3173574.3173768 · Crossref published 2018-04-19 · 90 citations (S2).

**MEASURED (from the publisher abstract, which I retrieved in full via Semantic Scholar):**
- **Web-based survey of 215 parents and their teens (ages 13–17)** — matched dyads.
- **Two separate logistic regression models** (one parent-reported, one teen-reported) predicting parental use of technical monitoring apps.
- Both models: **low autonomy-granting (authoritarian) parents were the most likely to use parental control apps.**
- Teen model additionally: **teens who were victimized online and had peer problems were MORE likely to be monitored.**
- **"Overall, increased parental control was associated with MORE (not fewer) online risks."**

**NOT REACHED:** the odds ratios, βs, pseudo-R², and per-predictor p-values. The PDF is gated: `dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/...` returns **403** even though Unpaywall/S2 label it GOLD OA; UCF STARS (scopus2015/7882) is metadata-only; NSF PAR has no record; fatcat.wiki was down (timeout); Marquette e-Publications returns a DOI link only. **The effect sizes for this finding are unverified.** Treat "restriction correlates with more risk" as MEASURED-but-cross-sectional (it is a correlation in a single-wave dyadic survey; the authors themselves do not claim direction).

---

### 1.3 Independent replication/extension of the review analysis — FULL TEXT READ

**Wang, G., Zhao, J., Van Kleek, M., Shadbolt, N. (2021). "Protection or Punishment? Relating the Design Space of Parental Control Apps and Perceptions About Them to Support Parenting for Online Safety." PACM HCI 5, CSCW2, Article 343, October 2021, 26pp.**
- DOI https://doi.org/10.1145/3476084 · arXiv **2109.05347** (2021-09-11) · full PDF: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.05347 · 46 citations
- Explicitly "replicating the method by Wisniewski et al. [75]"

**MEASURED:**
- **58 top Android parental control apps** analysed (including child-side app versions).
- Scraped **93,404 reviews**; after de-duplication **81,488**; after bigram/NLTK feature-mention filtering + two-author manual verification, **3,264 reviews** retained.
- Of those 3,264: **746 child reviews** and **2,518 parent reviews** (identified with the same "my mom / my dad / I'm a kid / I'm xx years old" keyword approach as Ghosh et al.).
- Three axes of feature variation found: **granularity, feedback/transparency, parent–child communications support.**
- Finding restated: children "mainly felt these apps were overly restrictive and invasive of their privacy and negatively impacting" the parent-child relationship; all-or-nothing (coarsest) controls least desirable because they "denied children access to basic, risk-free, and unproblematic aspects of their devices."
- **NOT REACHED:** this paper does not report a child-vs-parent mean-star comparison, so it does not independently replicate the 1.79-vs-3.66 gap.

---

### 1.4 Circle of Trust — FOUND, and it is CHI **2020**, not 2018

**Ghosh, A.K., Hughes, C.E., Wisniewski, P.J. (2020). "Circle of Trust: A New Approach to Mobile Online Safety for Families." CHI 2020, Honolulu, April 25–30, 2020.** DOI https://doi.org/10.1145/3313831.3376747
- Free PDF: **https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10213815** (NSF PAR 10213815)
- (Note: DOI 10.1145/3173574.3174200, sometimes miscited for this, is actually a Vertanen et al. virtual-keyboard paper.)

**MEASURED:** mixed-methods **within-subjects user study of 17 parent–child pairs**; power analysis stated a sample of **15** was needed (Power = 80%). Parents *and* children **significantly preferred** the Circle of Trust design over a baseline traditional parental-control app on perceived usefulness, ease of use, and behavioral intent to use; Circle of Trust rated **significantly less privacy-invasive for children**. Value-Sensitive-Design lens: families valuing **privacy, trust, freedom, balance of power** preferred the new app; families valuing **transparency and control** preferred the status quo. **Better suited to teens than younger children.**

---

### 1.5 Other measured work in this line (all reachable)

| Work | Locator | MEASURED content |
|---|---|---|
| **Akter, Godfrey, Kropczynski, Lipford, Wisniewski (2022), "From Parental Control to Joint Family Oversight" PACM HCI (CSCW1), doi 10.1145/3512904** | PDF https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10374187 | In-depth user study, **19 parent–teen (13–17) pairs**, CO-oPS community-oversight app. Transparency into one another's devices valued; co-management "beneficial but also quite difficult to implement in practice." |
| **Badillo-Urquiola, Wisniewski et al. (2020), "Beyond Parental Control: Designing Adolescent Online Safety Apps Using Value Sensitive Design," J. Adolescent Research, doi 10.1177/0743558419884692** | PDF https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10133416 · 53 citations | Value-Sensitive-Design study of teen-centric safety app design. |
| **Park, Akter, Wisniewski, Badillo-Urquiola (2024), "It's Still Complicated: From Privacy-Invasive Parental Control to Teen-Centric Solutions for Digital Resilience," IEEE Security & Privacy, doi 10.1109/MSEC.2024.3417804** | PDF https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10530845 | Position/overview by the same lab: "**relying solely on restrictive approaches to online safety may be ineffective in protecting adolescents from online risks, as it limits the potential opportunities for youth to interact with others online**"; names Life360 and Bark by name as the restrictive-mediation exemplars. |
| **Stoilova, M., Bulger, M., Livingstone, S. (2023), "Do parental control tools fulfil family expectations for child protection? A rapid evidence review," Journal of Children and Media, published online 2023-10-29, doi 10.1080/17482798.2023.2265512** | Abstract read at https://researchonline.lse.ac.uk/id/eprint/120219/ · 65 citations | **1,656 articles keyword-searched across 5 databases; 40 studies full-text coded.** Verdict: "**the consequences of use were mixed, with evidence of parental controls having both beneficial and adverse outcomes, limiting other outcomes or simply having no outcomes**"; "the review found **little support for advocating parental controls as a stand-alone strategy**." **NOT REACHED:** the per-outcome counts (how many of the 40 fell in each bucket) — the LSE eprints "full text" file is a 2-page cover sheet only, T&F returns 403, and the Wayback snapshot has no body. |
| **Nouwen, M. & Zaman, B.** — the two papers you asked for, by DOI (abstracts only, bodies NOT REACHED): "A value sensitive design approach to parental software for young children," IDC 2015, **doi 10.1145/2771839.2771917** (36 cites); "Redefining the role of parents in young children's online interactions. A value-sensitive design case study," Int. J. Child-Computer Interaction, Nov 2018, **doi 10.1016/j.ijcci.2018.06.001** (34 cites). |
| **Ko / Choi — FamiLync**, UbiComp 2015, **doi 10.1145/2750858.2804283**, 98 citations. Participatory parental-mediation system for smartphone use. Body NOT REACHED. |
| **Maier, E-M., & Tanczer, L.M. (2025), "Surveillance Disguised as Protection: A Comparative Analysis of Sideloaded and In-Store Parental Control Apps," arXiv 2504.16087 (2025-03-08)** | https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.16087 | See §3.4 below — this is the strongest recent *measured* security work on the app category. |

**Note on a related paper you may have been thinking of:** Alelyani, Ghosh, Moralez, Guha, Wisniewski (2019), "Examining Parent Versus Child Reviews of Parental Control Apps on Google Play" — this is the machine-learning follow-up to the 2018 paper. Cited as ref [4] in Wang et al. 2021. I did not reach its full text.

