The report was already finished and delivered in my previous message; I've now relayed a condensed version to the requesting agent. Nothing was cut short.

**What landed, in one line each:**

1. **The "1.7 stars" paper is real and I read the full PDF.** Ghosh et al., CHI 2018, doi `10.1145/3173574.3173698` — 736 child-authored reviews of 37 apps (from 29,272 scraped reviews of 66 apps, Aug 2016), ages 8–19. **M = 1.79 (SD 1.48) for children vs 3.66 (SD 1.79) for parents**, t(793.34)=33.77. **76% one-star.** Complaints coded: Overly Restrictive 35%, Privacy Invasive 23%, Bad Parenting 14%, Faulty Design 14%. Only free copy: `https://www.cs.ucf.edu/icerc/isuelab/publications/pubs/pn1838-ghoshA.pdf`

2. **A load-bearing negative result:** the word **"abuse" occurs zero times** in that paper. The "teens call it abuse" framing is not sourced there. The paper's own language is "parental stalking," and teen quotes using "spyware" and "evil."

3. **The restriction→more-risk finding is real but its effect sizes are NOT REACHED.** N=215 dyads, two logistic regressions, "increased parental control was associated with more (not fewer) online risks" — but every route to the PDF 403s, so no ORs or p-values.

4. **The LGBTQ+ forced-outing causal link does not exist as measured research** anywhere I could reach. The preconditions are heavily measured (Trevor N=28,524: 38% affirming home; HRC N=12,615: 40.9% out to all parents; GLSEN N=5,680: LGBT youth 3× as likely to not feel safe at home). The bridge is not.

5. **Warford's SoK actively declines to name the parent-as-adversary case.** It has the exact vocabulary — "implicit or explicit authority over the target" plus physical device access — and assigns it to intimate-partner abuse, while filing children/teens under a separate factor defined as third parties with "helpful or safety-focused reasons for 'privacy invasions'." It cites both Ghosh CHI 2018 papers and codes it that way anyway.

6. **Life360 is fully documented,** including the correct URL (slug ends `-user`, singular — the plural 404s), the $693K→$16M revenue arc, and the fact that the 2023 class action was brought by **a minor and his family**, with the complaint naming LGBTQ+-inferable locations.

Two tooling notes for whoever picks this up: **OpenAlex hit its free daily budget mid-session** and resets at midnight UTC, and the two routes that actually yielded gated PDFs were `par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/<id>` for NSF-funded work and university lab publication pages.
